<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Design the league from its failure cases backwards.</strong> Decide what happens when a fixture cannot be played, a team is incomplete, a player appears in the wrong side, a result is disputed or a team disappears. Once those rules exist, divisions and tables are comparatively easy.</p>

      <p>This guide is aimed at local inter-club competition rather than a national governing-body event. The examples are informed by current English local-league rules, but they are examples, not a model rulebook: Coventry, Exeter and Solihull already differ substantially in eligibility, promotion, postponement and match structure.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:28px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#purpose">Decide what the league is for</a>
      <a href="#numbers">Let the calendar choose division size</a>
      <a href="#format">Specify a match completely</a>
      <a href="#eligibility">Player eligibility is where loopholes live</a>
      <a href="#fixtures">Build fixtures around real venues</a>
      <a href="#postponement">Write a postponement rule people can obey</a>
      <a href="#results">Make the result process boring</a>
      <a href="#table">Points, tie-breaks and false incentives</a>
      <a href="#withdrawal">Plan for incomplete teams and withdrawals</a>
      <a href="#promotion">Promotion is also division engineering</a>
      <a href="#governance">Separate rules from discretion</a>
      <a href="#data">Where club data helps - and where it does not</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="purpose">Decide what the league is for</h2>

      <p>A league can optimise several different things:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>competitive matches between similar-strength teams;</li>
        <li>regular inter-club participation;</li>
        <li>a clear pathway from lower to higher divisions;</li>
        <li>meaningful opportunities for developing players;</li>
        <li>geographically local competition with manageable travel;</li>
        <li>or simply giving existing clubs a season-long structure.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>These goals occasionally conflict. Aggressive promotion and relegation improves sorting but creates churn. Strict player nomination protects divisions but can make it difficult to field a team when three people are unavailable. Large divisions create a fuller competition but increase venue and travel demands.</p>

      <p>Write the league's priorities down. They become the principles for resolving rules you did not predict.</p>

      <h2 id="numbers">Let the calendar choose division size</h2>

      <p>Do not begin with "a good division has eight teams". Begin with how many match dates a team can actually sustain.</p>

      <p>In a double round robin, each team plays:</p>

      <p style="text-align:center;font-size:1.08rem"><strong>2 × (teams in division − 1)</strong></p>

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Teams in division</th><th>Matches per team, home & away</th><th>Total matches in division</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>5</td><td>8</td><td>20</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6</td><td>10</td><td>30</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7</td><td>12</td><td>42</td></tr>
            <tr><td>8</td><td>14</td><td>56</td></tr>
            <tr><td>10</td><td>18</td><td>90</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:18px">Current Coventry rules say divisions should contain six or seven teams where possible. That is a useful real-world example, not evidence of an optimal number. The reason is operational: enough opponents for a season without creating an impossible fixture burden.</p>

      <p>For a new league, a single round robin, central venue or shorter pilot can be wiser than copying the full home-and-away structure of an established competition on day one.</p>

      <h2 id="format">Specify a match completely</h2>

      <p>"Six doubles" is not a complete format.</p>

      <p>A rulebook should define:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>team size and any gender composition;</li>
        <li>which pairs play which opposing pairs;</li>
        <li>best of three or another scoring structure;</li>
        <li>what happens if time expires mid-rubber;</li>
        <li>whether substitutions are permitted and when;</li>
        <li>shuttle requirements and who supplies them;</li>
        <li>minimum team size before a match is considered conceded;</li>
        <li>the treatment of an unplayed rubber;</li>
        <li>start-time / continuous-play obligations;</li>
        <li>and who decides disputed line calls or conduct issues when there is no umpire.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Local rules differ sharply. Coventry, for example, has separate men's, mixed and ladies structures and explicit consequences for delay; Solihull has its own six-rubber structures and incomplete-team provisions. That variation is exactly why a new league should design from its local players and venue reality rather than copying isolated clauses.</p>

      <h2 id="eligibility">Player eligibility is where loopholes live</h2>

      <p>If one club enters several teams at different levels, somebody will eventually ask whether a strong first-team player can help the fourth team on a difficult night.</p>

      <p>The rules need an answer before that person is standing in the hall.</p>

      <p>Common mechanisms include:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>pre-season nominations:</strong> named stronger players cannot play down;</li>
        <li><strong>team order / merit lists:</strong> squads have a declared hierarchy;</li>
        <li><strong>play-up limits:</strong> after a player represents a higher side a set number of times, they become tied higher;</li>
        <li><strong>transfer deadlines:</strong> restrict mid-season movement between clubs or teams;</li>
        <li><strong>committee standard powers:</strong> a backstop where a nominally legal player is clearly inappropriate for the division.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Current Coventry, Exeter and Solihull rules use different combinations of these ideas. Coventry has nominations and appearance thresholds; Exeter permits limited play-up before a player becomes tied; Solihull also combines nominations with appearance rules.</p>

      <p>For a new league, simpler is better. Every extra exception creates an administrative state somebody must track. But too little control makes "team" mean whoever a club can assemble that evening.</p>

      <h2 id="fixtures">Build fixtures around real venues</h2>

      <p>The mathematical fixture generator is the easy part. The constraint problem is harder:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Club A only hosts on Tuesdays.</li>
        <li>Club B has two teams sharing the same two courts.</li>
        <li>Club C loses its school hall during exams.</li>
        <li>Two teams from the same club should not both be away every week.</li>
        <li>Christmas, half-terms and cup weeks remove chunks of calendar.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Gather each team's home-night, venue availability and blackout dates before generating fixtures. Then leave deliberate slack near the end of the season for rearrangements.</p>

      <p>Published dates should have one canonical owner. A spreadsheet emailed in September and a website updated in January cannot both be the truth.</p>

      <p>If clubs are permitted to agree their own dates, set a deadline by which all fixtures must be lodged with the league. Coventry's current rules do exactly that: clubs arrange fixtures and submit the full list before matches proceed.</p>

      <h2 id="postponement">Write a postponement rule people can obey</h2>

      <p>"Matches may be rearranged by mutual agreement" sounds friendly until April contains eleven unplayed fixtures.</p>

      <p>Define:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>what counts as a valid reason;</li>
        <li>how quickly the opponent and secretary must be notified;</li>
        <li>who proposes replacement dates;</li>
        <li>the deadline for agreeing one;</li>
        <li>the final date by which it must be played;</li>
        <li>and what happens when clubs cannot agree.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Real leagues take quite different positions. Coventry generally restricts postponement to hall unavailability or exceptional circumstances with secretary permission; Solihull explicitly says player unavailability is not normally good cause.</p>

      <p>The stricter the rule, the more you should make sure ordinary clubs can realistically field teams under the eligibility rules. There is little value in a beautifully rigid fixture list that produces forfeits every February.</p>

      <h2 id="results">Make the result process boring</h2>

      <p>The league should receive one structured result containing enough information to recalculate everything it publishes.</p>

      <p>At minimum:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>fixture identity;</li>
        <li>date and venue;</li>
        <li>players / pairings, if eligibility is checked;</li>
        <li>each rubber score;</li>
        <li>overall match result;</li>
        <li>captain confirmation or dispute flag;</li>
        <li>submission timestamp.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Do not ask the secretary to retype a WhatsApp photo of a scorecard into a second system if you can avoid it.</p>

      <p>Set a reporting deadline and a correction window. Coventry currently makes the winner responsible for the scorecard (home team on a draw) and imposes a deadline; the exact penalty is its choice, but the principle is strong: <strong>results submission is part of the fixture, not optional paperwork afterwards.</strong></p>

      <h2 id="table">Points, tie-breaks and false incentives</h2>

      <p>There is no universally correct league-points system.</p>

      <p>You can rank by matches won, award points for win/draw/loss, award points by rubbers, or combine them. Each rewards something different.</p>

      <p>Before choosing, run hypothetical final tables. Ask:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Can a team benefit from conceding rather than playing short?</li>
        <li>Does a 6-0 win matter more than a 4-2 win, and should it?</li>
        <li>Can a team with fewer match wins top the table through bonus/rubber points?</li>
        <li>What happens when teams played unequal numbers because of withdrawals?</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Then write the tie-break sequence all the way to the end. Current Solihull rules, for example, go through matches, rubbers, games and points and eventually head-to-head criteria; Coventry uses its own sequence. There is no reason your league must copy either. There is every reason not to invent a tie-break after seeing which clubs it affects.</p>

      <h2 id="withdrawal">Plan for incomplete teams and withdrawals</h2>

      <p>A withdrawal halfway through the season is the rulebook's stress test.</p>

      <p>Suppose Team X has played five of seven opponents, including an upset win over the title favourite, then folds.</p>

      <p>You have three broad choices:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>expunge its results:</strong> mathematically restores equal opponent sets but erases games that happened;</li>
        <li><strong>retain completed results and award future concessions:</strong> preserves history but gives unplayed opponents a different type of result;</li>
        <li><strong>retain only a defined minimum / use averages:</strong> possible, but complexity rises quickly.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Coventry currently expunges results of a withdrawing team that has played matches and treats it as bottom of the division. Other leagues can choose differently. The key is that the decision is known before it changes the title race.</p>

      <p>Do the same for incomplete teams: define whether missing rubbers are conceded, what score is recorded and whether financial/league penalties are separate from the sporting result.</p>

      <h2 id="promotion">Promotion is also division engineering</h2>

      <p>"Top two up, bottom two down" works only while the number of teams remains stable.</p>

      <p>Then a club withdraws a team. A new club enters. Two clubs merge. A second team is added. One division has five teams and another eight. Suddenly normal promotion does not produce sensible divisions.</p>

      <p>Write a hierarchy:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>normal promotion/relegation;</li>
        <li>how vacancies are filled;</li>
        <li>how new teams are placed;</li>
        <li>limits on multiple teams from one club in a division;</li>
        <li>committee power to restructure divisions between seasons.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Make that restructuring power explicit but bounded. Clubs should know that league composition can be adjusted for viability without believing the committee can casually overturn competitive results.</p>

      <h2 id="governance">Separate rules from discretion</h2>

      <p>The secretary should administer the competition, not become its court of final appeal.</p>

      <p>For decisions that can materially affect standings - eligibility breaches, forfeits, conduct, exceptional postponements - define:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>who makes the first decision;</li>
        <li>whether that person must recuse themselves if their club is involved;</li>
        <li>what evidence is considered;</li>
        <li>the appeal route;</li>
        <li>the appeal deadline;</li>
        <li>and whether decisions / precedents are recorded.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>A short published decision note is often healthier than a private chain of emails. Next season's committee then knows how the same clause was interpreted last time.</p>

      <h2 id="data">Where club data helps - and where it does not</h2>

      <p>Player ratings can help a club choose teams and can give a league useful context when placing a completely new side. They are not a substitute for league results.</p>

      <p>In particular, an internal club rating may not be calibrated against another club's scale. A 1700 in Club A and a 1700 in Club B are not automatically equivalent unless the rating networks are connected.</p>

      <p>That limits how far a league secretary should take club-night data. Use it as evidence when seeding a new team, not as an allegedly objective cross-club ranking.</p>

      <p>On the club side, <a href="/guides/picking-league-teams-club-data">team-selection data</a> can still be valuable: results, player form and partnership records help captains make a better decision about who should represent each side. <a href="/features/league-team-support">ePegboard's league-team tools</a> sit on that club side of the boundary; they do not replace the league's fixture, eligibility and table administration.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>The secretary's real product</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">It is not the fixture list. It is <strong>a competition state every club believes</strong>: these players were eligible, these matches count, this is the table, these are the outstanding fixtures, and this is how any exception was decided.</p>
      </div>

